---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { getLocaleFromUrl } from '@/i18n/utils';
import { getBlogPost, blogChrome } from '@/i18n/blog';

const locale = getLocaleFromUrl(Astro.url, Astro.currentLocale);
const post = getBlogPost('how-to-create-vcard-qr-code', locale);
const chrome = blogChrome[locale];
---

<Base title={post.title} description={post.description} schema="faq" faqItems={post.faqItems}>
  <article class="blog-post">
    <div class="container">
      <nav class="breadcrumbs" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
        <a href="/">{chrome.home}</a> › <a href="/blog/">{chrome.blog}</a> › <span>{post.breadcrumb}</span>
      </nav>

      <header class="blog-header">
        <p class="blog-meta">{post.meta}</p>
        <h1>{post.h1}</h1>
        <p class="blog-lede">{post.lede}</p>
      </header>

      {post.bodyNote && (
        <p class="blog-body-note">{post.bodyNote}</p>
      )}

      <div class="blog-content">
        <p>Every paper business card you hand out has the same fate: a desk drawer, a wallet back pocket, or the recycling bin within a week. A vCard QR code changes that. One scan with a phone camera, and your contact lands directly in the recipient's address book — with every detail, spelled correctly, ready to dial or email.</p>

        <h2>What is a vCard?</h2>
        <p>vCard is an open standard file format for electronic business cards. It can store:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Name (first, last, middle, prefix, suffix)</li>
          <li>Organization and job title</li>
          <li>Phone numbers (multiple types: mobile, work, fax)</li>
          <li>Email addresses</li>
          <li>Physical address</li>
          <li>Website and social URLs</li>
          <li>Photo URL</li>
          <li>Notes</li>
        </ul>
        <p>When encoded in a QR code, the phone recognizes the format and offers to save the contact. The recipient never types anything.</p>

        <h2>Why use a QR code instead of just typing your details?</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Speed:</strong> One scan vs. typing 10+ fields</li>
          <li><strong>Accuracy:</strong> No typos in your email or phone number</li>
          <li><strong>Always up to date:</strong> You can include a link to your latest contact info, or rotate the QR code itself</li>
          <li><strong>Better conversion:</strong> People are far more likely to actually save the contact when it takes one tap</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>How to create a vCard QR code</h2>
        <ol>
          <li>Open the <a href="/vcard-qr-code">vCard QR Code Generator</a>.</li>
          <li>Fill in your <strong>name</strong> (first and last).</li>
          <li>Add your <strong>organization</strong> and <strong>title</strong>.</li>
          <li>Enter your <strong>phone</strong> (mobile is most useful).</li>
          <li>Enter your <strong>email</strong>.</li>
          <li>Add your <strong>website</strong> and any social URLs (LinkedIn, Twitter, etc.) — keep it short.</li>
          <li>Optionally add your address for in-person meetings.</li>
          <li>Choose an error correction level of <strong>Q (Quartile, 25%)</strong> — gives you room to add a logo overlay if you want.</li>
          <li>Download as <strong>SVG</strong> for printing or <strong>PNG</strong> for digital use.</li>
        </ol>

        <h2>Field-by-field best practices</h2>
        <h3>Name fields</h3>
        <p>Use your real name as it appears on official documents. Don't include honorifics (Dr., Mr.) unless you use them professionally — saves QR space.</p>
        <h3>Phone numbers</h3>
        <p>Include the country code (+1, +44, +86). Mobile is most useful; include work only if relevant.</p>
        <h3>Email</h3>
        <p>Use your most-reachable email. Avoid info@ aliases — they look impersonal and tend to be ignored.</p>
        <h3>URLs</h3>
        <p>Limit to 1-2 critical links. LinkedIn + personal site is the sweet spot. Don't dump every social profile you have.</p>
        <h3>Address</h3>
        <p>Include only if people actually visit you. For most remote workers, skip it.</p>

        <h2>How big should the QR code be on a business card?</h2>
        <p>A standard business card is 3.5 × 2 inches (85 × 55 mm). The QR code should be at least <strong>0.8 × 0.8 inches</strong> (20 × 20 mm) — preferably larger. Place it on the back of the card or in a corner of the front, leaving at least a 4-module white border around it.</p>

        <h2>Common mistakes to avoid</h2>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Too many fields.</strong> If your QR code has more than 15 fields, it gets hard to scan. Less is more.</li>
          <li><strong>No white space around the code.</strong> Printers and designers often crop margins. Always leave a "quiet zone" of at least 4 modules of white space around the code.</li>
          <li><strong>Logo overlay without enough error correction.</strong> If you put a logo in the middle, use H (30%) error correction, otherwise the code breaks.</li>
          <li><strong>Outdated info.</strong> If you change jobs, regenerate the QR code immediately — paper cards already printed will save the old info.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>{chrome.faqHeading}</h2>
        {post.faqItems.map((f) => (
          <details>
            <summary>{f.question}</summary>
            <p>{f.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}

        <p class="cta">Create your own vCard QR code in 60 seconds. <a href="/vcard-qr-code">Generate a vCard QR code →</a></p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </article>
</Base>

<style>
  .blog-post { padding: var(--space-lg) 0; }
  .breadcrumbs { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-md); font-size: 0.875rem; color: var(--color-fg-soft); }
  .breadcrumbs a { color: var(--color-fg-soft); }
  .breadcrumbs a:hover { color: var(--color-accent-strong); }
  .blog-header { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-lg); text-align: center; }
  .blog-meta { color: var(--color-fg-soft); font-size: 0.875rem; margin-bottom: var(--space-sm); }
  .blog-header h1 { font-size: 2.25rem; line-height: 1.2; }
  .blog-lede { font-size: 1.125rem; color: var(--color-fg-soft); max-width: 640px; margin: var(--space-md) auto 0; }
  .blog-body-note {
    max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto var(--space-md);
    padding: var(--space-sm) var(--space-md);
    background: var(--color-accent-soft);
    color: var(--color-accent-strong);
    border-left: 3px solid var(--color-accent);
    border-radius: var(--radius-sm);
    font-size: 0.9rem;
  }
  .blog-content { max-width: 720px; margin: 0 auto; }
  .blog-content h2 { margin-top: var(--space-xl); }
  .blog-content h3 { margin-top: var(--space-lg); }
  .blog-content p, .blog-content li { font-size: 1.0625rem; line-height: 1.7; }
  .blog-content details {
    margin: var(--space-sm) 0; padding: var(--space-md);
    border: 1px solid var(--color-border); border-radius: var(--radius-md);
    background: linear-gradient(180deg, var(--color-bg) 0%, var(--color-bg-soft) 100%);
  }
  .blog-content summary { cursor: pointer; font-weight: 600; }
  .blog-content .cta { margin-top: var(--space-xl); padding: var(--space-md); background: var(--color-accent-soft); border-radius: var(--radius-md); text-align: center; }
  .blog-content .cta a { font-weight: 600; }
</style>